  - name: Kings honoured
    value: Ptolemy III Euergetes, Ptolemy IV Philopator, Ptolemy V Epiphanes and
      Ptolemy VI
  - name: One of the copies
    value: The Rosetta Stone, found 1799
pull-quotes: []
timeline:
  - when: 305 BC to 30 BC
    what: The Ptolemaic Kingdom controls Egypt.
  - when: 13th of Gorpiaios (3 December) 243 BCE
    what: The Decree of Alexandria orders statues of Ptolemy III and Berenice II,
      and a shrine for them, in each temple.
  - when: Thursday 7 March 238 BCE
    what: The Decree of Canopus is issued, dated 7 Appellaios (Mac.) = 17 Tybi
      (Eg.) in year 9 of Ptolemy III.
  - when: 217 BC
    what: The Raphia Decree is issued under Ptolemy IV.
  - when: 196 BC
    what: The Decree of Memphis is issued under Ptolemy V; one copy of it is the
      Rosetta Stone.
  - when: 6 September 186 BC
    what: The Philensis II Decree is issued at Alexandria after the suppression of
      Ankhwennefer's Upper Egyptian revolt.
  - when: 9 October 186 BC
    what: The New Year's Decree, perhaps to celebrate the birth of Ptolemy VI.
  - when: 29 October 185 BC
    what: The Philensis I Decree is issued at Memphis upon the enthronement of an
      Apis bull.
  - when: 29 May 182 BC
    what: A decree follows a visit to Memphis by the Mnevis bull.
  - when: 31 July 161 BC
    what: A decree marks a royal visit to Memphis, shortly after Ptolemy VI was
      restored to power.
  - when: "1799"
    what: The Rosetta Stone is found.
  - when: "1866"
    what: The first Stele of Canopus is found.
  - when: "1881"
    what: A second Stele of Canopus is found.
  - when: early 1880s
    what: The Nubayrah Stele is found; its lines 1–27 are used to complete the
      missing lines on the Rosetta Stone.
  - when: "1902"
    what: The Stele of Memphis (CGC 31088), carrying the Raphia Decree, is found
      at the site of ancient Memphis.
  - when: "1923"
    what: The Pithom Stele, No. II (CGC 50048) is found at Tell el-Maskhuta.
  - when: between 1934-36
    what: The Fragment from Tod is found, and disappears shortly after.
  - when: "2004"
    what: A fourth text of the Decree of Canopus is discovered at Bubastis by the
      German-Egyptian 'Tell Basta Project'.
image-plan:
  - file: RosettaStoneDetail.jpg
    role: hero
    section: lead
    caption: "The Rosetta Stone, found in 1799, carries the Decree of Memphis of
      196 BC: 14 lines of hieroglyphs where more once stood, 32 lines of
      Demotic, 54 lines of Greek capitals, cut in dark granite."
  - file: Kanopos_Kararnamesi.jpg
    role: inline
    section: 238-bc-decree-of-canopus-ptolemy-iii
    caption: "The Stele of Canopus found in 1866: 37 lines of hieroglyphs, 74
      lines of Demotic down the right side and 76 lines of Greek capitals, in
      fine limestone."
  - file: Tell_el-Maskhutah_Raphia_Decree_Stele.jpg
    role: inline
    section: 217-bc-raphia-decree-ptolemy-iv
    caption: "The Raphia decree, found at Memphis. Two other witnesses to the text
      are known: the Pithom Stele from Tell el-Maskhuta, and a fragment from
      Tod that vanished shortly after it was found."
glossary:
  - term: synod
    gloss: The assembly of ancient Egyptian priests that issued each of these
      decrees.
  - term: stele
    gloss: An upright stone slab; multiple copies of each decree were inscribed on
      steles and erected in temple courtyards.
  - term: Demotic
    gloss: One of the three scripts the decrees were cut in, alongside hieroglyphs
      and Greek.
  - term: "'capitals'"
    gloss: The majuscule Greek letters in which the Greek version of a decree was
      inscribed.
  - term: proleptic Julian calendar
    gloss: "The reckoning used to turn a decree's Macedonian and Egyptian dates
      into a single day: Canopus's 7 Appellaios and 17 Tybi become Thursday 7
      March 238 BCE."
  - term: beneficent gods
    gloss: The standing the Decree of Alexandria gave Ptolemy III and Berenice II,
      to be worshipped as such at a shrine in every temple.
  - term: Apis bull
    gloss: A bull whose enthronement was the occasion for the Philensis I Decree,
      issued at Memphis on 29 October 185 BC.
  - term: Mnevis bull
    gloss: A bull whose visit to Memphis was followed by the decree of 29 May 182
      BC.
further:
  - slug: rosetta-stone
    why: The stone found in 1799 that carries one copy of the 196 BC Decree of
      Memphis.
  - slug: rosetta-stone-decree
    why: The Decree of Memphis itself, the one Ptolemy V decree of which two and a
      half copies of the text survive.
  - slug: ancient-egyptian-religion
    why: The priesthoods whose synods issued the decrees, and whose support the
      Ptolemies were paying for.
