The !!Ptolemaic Decrees!! all work the same way. A [[ synods | synod ]] of [[ ancient Egyptian priests | ancient-egyptian-religion ]] meets and sets down the benefactions of the reigning [[ pharaoh ]], especially those towards the priesthood, and religious honours are decreed for him in return. They were issued in the [[ Ptolemaic Kingdom ]], which controlled [[ Egypt | ancient-egypt ]] from 305 BC to 30 BC.[[ ^1 ]]

Two came under [[ Ptolemy III Euergetes ]] (the Decree of Alexandria and [[ Decree of Canopus ]]), another under [[ Ptolemy IV Philopator ]] (the [[ Raphia Decree ]]), and others under [[ Ptolemy V Epiphanes ]] (the [[ Decree of Memphis | rosetta-stone-decree ]] and the two Philensis Decrees). None of them was meant to exist in a single copy: multiple copies, inscribed on stone [[ steles | stele ]], were erected in temple [[ courtyards | courtyard ]], as specified in the text of the decrees themselves.[[ ^2 ]]

What has come down is less than that. There exist three copies plus a fragment of the Decree of Canopus, two copies of the Memphis Decree (one imperfect), and two and a half copies of the text of the [[ Rosetta Stone ]], including the copy on the [[ Nubayrah Stele ]] and a temple wall inscription with edits, or scene replacements, completed by subsequent scribes.

== 243 BC Decree of Alexandria (Ptolemy III)

The Decree of Alexandria was issued on the 13th of Gorpiaios (3 December) 243 BCE. It proclaimed that statues of Ptolemy III and his wife [[ Berenice II ]], together with a shrine for them, should be set up in each temple, so that the two could be worshipped as beneficent gods.[[ ^3 ]] The reasons cited for these honours include the king's services to Egypt in general, but above all his support for the temples; it is therefore possible that the decree was intended to strike a balance between the Greek monarch and the still powerful priesthoods.[[ ^4 ]]

== 238 BC Decree of Canopus (Ptolemy III)

The decree was issued on 7 Appellaios (Mac.) = 17 Tybi (Eg.) year 9 of Ptolemy III = Thursday 7 March 238 BCE (proleptic Julian calendar).

- Stone 1: !!Stele of Canopus!!, (no. 1), found 1866, 37 lines of hieroglyphs, 74 lines of [[ Demotic | demotic-egyptian ]] (right side), 76 lines of Greek [[ 'capitals' | majuscule ]], fine limestone.
- Stone 2: !![[ Stele of Canopus | decree-of-canopus ]]!! (no. 2), found 1881, 26 lines hieroglyphs, 20 lines Demotic, 64 lines Greek 'capitals', white limestone.
- 3rd partial text with lines of hieroglyphs (now in the [[ Louvre ]]).
- 4th text was discovered in 2004 at [[ Bubastis ]], by the German-Egyptian 'Tell Basta Project'.

== 217 BC Raphia Decree (Ptolemy IV)

The decree of [[ Ptolemy IV Philopator ]] is carried by three stones, two of them still to be seen and one known only from photographs.

- Stone 1: !!Stele of Memphis!! (CGC 31088), found in 1902 at the site of ancient Memphis, hieroglyphs, Demotic, and Greek, dark granite.
- Stone 2: !!Pithom Stele, No. II!! (CGC 50048), found in 1923 at [[ Tell el-Maskhuta ]], hieroglyphs (front), 42 lines Demotic (back), virtually complete, providing an almost total translation, and Greek (side), sandstone.
- Stone 3: !!Fragment from Tod!!, found between 1934-36, disappearing shortly after discovery; it was published on photographic evidence alone.[[ ^5 ]]

== 196 BC Decree of Memphis (Ptolemy V)

- Stone 1: !!The [[ Rosetta Stone ]]!!, found 1799, (remaining) hieroglyphs, 14 lines, 32 lines Demotic, 54 lines Greek [[ 'capitals' | majuscule ]], dark granite ([[ granodiorite ]]).
- Stone 2: !![[ Nubayrah Stele ]]!!, found in the early 1880s, hieroglyphs, lines 1–27 were used to complete the missing lines on the Rosetta Stone, Demotic, Greek capitals, limestone.
- Site 3: the Temple of [[ Philae ]], inscribed hieroglyphs from the Third Decree on walls, also overwritten, with scenes and figures of humans/gods.

== 186 BC Philensis II Decree (Ptolemy V)

Issued at Alexandria on 6 September 186 BC, after the suppression of [[ Ankhwennefer's | ankhwennefer-pharaoh ]] Upper Egyptian revolt.[[ ^2 ]][[ ^6 ]] Two steles carry it.

- Stone 1: //Stele of Philae II//
- Stone 2: //Stele of Cairo//

== 186 BC New Year's Decree (Ptolemy V)

Issued on 9 October 186 BC, New Year's day, perhaps to celebrate the birth of [[ Ptolemy VI ]].[[ ^6 ]] What survives of it is not stone but papyrus.

- Papyrus 1: //P. Kroll. = P. Köln// 7.313

== 185 BC Philensis I Decree (Ptolemy V)

Issued at Memphis on 29 October 185 BC, upon the enthronement of an [[ Apis bull ]].[[ ^2 ]][[ ^6 ]]

- Stone 1: //Stele of Philae I//

== 182 BC Memphis decree (Ptolemy V)

Issued on 29 May 182 BC, after a visit to Memphis by the [[ Mnevis bull ]].[[ ^6 ]]

== 161 BC Decree (Ptolemy VI)

Issued on 31 July 161 BC, upon a royal visit to Memphis, shortly after [[ Ptolemy VI ]] was restored to power.[[ ^6 ]]

- Stone 1: Cairo Stele 22184.

